# oe-topics.env — the ONE definition of what gets archived, and of which job archives it.
#
# Sourced by oe-archive-kafka.sh (topic defaults), oe-archive-daily.sh (nightly wrapper),
# oe-archive-dev.sh (2-hourly dev capture) and oe-archive-verify.sh (completeness check).
# Previously each carried its own copy of the same names: equal today, free to drift tomorrow —
# and two environments archiving different topic sets do not produce a comparison, they produce two
# unrelated datasets that merely look comparable. Codex flagged exactly that on 2026-08-08:
# "current equality, not structural prevention".
#
# Change a set HERE and every caller changes with it. Adding a topic is safe (the archiver skips
# topics that do not exist); removing one silently ends its capture, so remove deliberately.
DEALER_LEDGER_EVIDENCE="dealer-ledger-signal-fired dealer-ledger-outcome-scored dealer-ledger-state dealer-ledger-profile dealer-ledger-calibration-state"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JOB SPLIT — why these two sets are DISJOINT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-12: the daily-heavy archive produced nothing for env=prod on 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11
# while the 10-minute spot archive stayed current. Both called oe-archive-kafka.sh with ENV=prod and
# the same ARCHIVE_DIR, so both computed the SAME lock path (/tmp/oe-archive-kafka.prod.<hash>.lock).
# The 10-minute job held it at 17:10; the daily job logged "exiting clean" and returned rc=0.
# Nothing alerted, because exit 0 was being read as proof of archival.
#
# The lock itself was right: two writers into one <topic>.offsets checkpoint file can each advance
# past a range the other never wrote, and that is a GAP. The defect was that the lock's SCOPE was
# the whole environment when its real subject is a TOPIC.
#
# Fixed in two independent layers, either of which alone would have prevented this:
#   1. POLICY (here): the two jobs own disjoint topic sets, so they have nothing to serialise on.
#   2. MECHANISM (oe-archive-kafka.sh): locks are per job AND per topic, so an ad-hoc run with an
#      overlapping list still cannot corrupt a manifest — and cannot starve a scheduled job either.
# Keep these sets disjoint. If they must overlap, layer 2 keeps it correct, but the overlapping
# topic gets skipped by whichever job arrives second and only layer 1 guarantees promptness.

# SPOT — owned by the */10 cron (ARCHIVE_JOB=spot).
# These must be archived far more often than once a day: underlying.spx.price is
# cleanup.policy=compact,delete with min.cleanable.dirty.ratio=0.01 and segment.ms=1h, so a closed
# segment is compacted almost at once — on 2026-08-03 only 3,306 of 804,629 offsets were still
# readable. Only the ACTIVE segment is safe from the cleaner.
OE_SPOT_TOPICS="underlying.spx.price underlying.spx.index.price underlying.es.price spx.basis.state"

# HEAVY — owned by the 17:10 ET daily wrapper (ARCHIVE_JOB=daily). Multi-million-record topics
# that are written once per session and are pointless to poll every 10 minutes.
#
# 2026-08-12 additions: delta-flow-by-strike and option-price-behavior-by-strike. These are the two
# primary inputs to near-strike bias (NEAR-STRIKE-BIAS-GATE1.md) and they were archived NOWHERE, in
# any environment, against a 1-day Kafka retention — every session of both was being destroyed
# nightly. Both are plain JSON on 32 partitions and carry their own tradingDate.
OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod="options.databento.events.raw options.databento.display options.databento.gex.strike options.databento.gex.strike.history delta-flow-by-strike option-price-behavior-by-strike close.direction.signal underlying.vix.index.price underlying.vix.price $DEALER_LEDGER_EVIDENCE"

# The full prod policy = what a complete session looks like. The verifier checks THIS set; the two
# jobs above partition it. A topic that is in neither job but in this set would alert every day,
# which is the correct way to notice that a topic has no producer job.
OE_ALL_TOPICS_prod="$OE_SPOT_TOPICS $OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod"

# ES4 — owned by the 17:01 ET post-close cron (ARCHIVE_JOB=es4) on the prod host, reading the
# es4 broker directly; the crontab passes this set via TOPICS. Defined HERE for the same reason
# as everything above: one definition, every caller.
# 2026-08-15 additions: es.futures.cvd.bars and es.futures.cvd (ES-CVD-DESIGN.md, Gates 1-4).
#   es.futures.cvd.bars — compacted with a UNIQUE key per bar (symbol|timeframe|barStartMs), so
#     compaction is not the threat; es4's clean-reset log wipe is (the 2026-08-11 loss class).
#     This is the CVD history: one record per closed bar per timeframe, all closed by 17:00, so
#     the 17:01 capture holds exactly one whole session.
#   es.futures.cvd (the 1 Hz snapshot) is deliberately NOT here. Measured 2026-08-15 on the
#     first capture attempt: the archiver bounds a read by --max-messages == offset span and
#     relies on a 60s idle timeout to finish early — a compacted topic with a LIVE 1 Hz producer
#     satisfies neither (the span is unreachable through compaction gaps, and it never goes
#     idle), so the consumer starves until the 900s kill (rc=124) and the topic fails EVERY run,
#     forever — a daily false alarm, which is how real alarms die. Its only archival value (the
#     terminal session state) is carried by the session-timeframe bar in cvd.bars anyway.
OE_ES4_TOPICS="es.underlying.es.trades es.tape-zones.cells es.futures.cvd.bars"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPLETENESS FLOORS — records per topic per trading date, below which a date is PARTIAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Format: "<topic>:<min_records> ..." — anything unlisted defaults to OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT.
#
# These are FLOORS, not expectations: set at roughly a tenth of an observed normal session, so a
# truncated or half-written date trips the alarm while a genuinely quiet session does not. Observed
# counts (2026-08-07, a full session) are in the comments so the next person can see what a normal
# day looks like rather than re-deriving it.
#
# ⚠️ The two feature topics are floored DELIBERATELY LOW — presence checks, not volume checks — and
# both MUST be re-derived from the first clean single-session archive. Measured 2026-08-12 on the
# first-ever capture of each:
#   delta-flow-by-strike            cleanup.policy=delete   → 873,382 records, full history intact
#   option-price-behavior-by-strike cleanup.policy=compact,delete
#                                   → 138,554 readable of 16,564,067 offsets: 0.836% SURVIVED
# OPB's history is destroyed by COMPACTION before any archiver can see it (p0: 61 readable of
# 417,095). A daily run captures mostly the active segment, which the cleaner has not touched yet,
# so its per-session yield is unknown until a full session has run — and guessing a floor now would
# either page every morning or check nothing at all. See ARCHIVE-FAIL-SILENT-FIX-20260812.md §6.
#
# A floor of 0 means OPTIONAL: this topic may legitimately produce nothing on a given day, and its
# absence is reported as EMPTY rather than failing the date. Use it only where silence is a real
# state of the world, never to quieten a topic you have not diagnosed — an alarm nobody trusts is
# how the original defect survived four evenings. The dealer-ledger fire topics qualify: verified on
# 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11, prod produced zero signal-fired and zero outcome-scored records on both
# days. Those are days on which no signal met threshold, not days on which archiving failed.
OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT="${OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_DEFAULT:-1}"
OE_ARCHIVE_MIN_RECORDS="\
dealer-ledger-signal-fired:0 \
dealer-ledger-outcome-scored:0 \
dealer-ledger-calibration-state:0 \
dealer-ledger-state:1000 \
dealer-ledger-profile:1000 \
close.direction.signal:1 \
options.databento.events.raw:500000 \
options.databento.display:300000 \
options.databento.gex.strike:400000 \
options.databento.gex.strike.history:150000 \
delta-flow-by-strike:1000 \
option-price-behavior-by-strike:100 \
underlying.spx.index.price:5000 \
underlying.es.price:20000 \
underlying.spx.price:1000 \
spx.basis.state:100 \
underlying.vix.index.price:50 \
es.futures.cvd.bars:500 \
underlying.vix.price:100"
